extraction_notes: |
  - Underseas, printed 164-165. The horune are sea-going pirates and minor
    supernatural beings, and the last playable class in this book.
  - ITS PSIONICS ARE A REAL GRANT, unlike almost every other race here. Printed
    164 makes every horune a MAJOR psionic of vast ability with five named
    powers - hydrokinesis, object read, mind block, resist fatigue, resist hunger
    - plus three physical powers of choice. Hydrokinesis is a SUPER row in this
    catalog and is granted by name, which is why `categories_allowed` is Physical
    alone: the three chosen powers come from there, and the super power is a
    grant rather than a pick.
  - THE STORED COMBAT BONUSES ARE THE SUM OF A RACIAL SET AND AN UNDERWATER SET,
    and this is the first class in this book where the two are printed
    separately and both apply. The racial bonuses hold everywhere; the underwater
    ones add on top. Summing them and stating the land subtraction in the
    restrictions is the same call made on the Amphib and the naut''yll, applied to
    a case where the book itself does the splitting.
  - FOUR OF ITS SKILLS ARE PRINTED WITH NO BONUS AT ALL - underwater navigation,
    undersea salvage, undersea survival and the four W.P.s. Their stored bases
    are the bare catalog figures, which is worth stating because every other
    class in this book decorates almost every line with a percentage, and a
    missing bonus reads like a transcription slip. It is not; printed 164 gives
    those three no parenthetical.
  - THE PILOT BONUS IS TWO NUMBERS AND ONE IS STORED. Printed 164 gives Pilot:
    Any (+5%; +10% on all seacraft). A category entry carries one bonus for every
    skill in it, so the general +5% is stored and the seacraft half is in the
    note - the same trade made on the Dragon Ray''s Domestic line.
  - MYSTICS ARE F23(a) AGAIN, the fifth appearance in this book: a horune mystic
    takes the mystic O.C.C.''s powers plus these skills and NO related skills at
    all. Added to F23(a)''s affected rows.
